    detail: {"dayInLife": ["Most carpenters are on-site early, reading the day's plans before the first cut. Framing crews rough in walls, floors, and roof systems; finish carpenters hang doors, run trim, and build the details people actually see and touch.", "It is hands-on, physical, and different every day. You move between the tape measure, the saw, and the level constantly, and by the end of a job you can point at a building and say you built that. The work runs across residential, commercial, and industrial sites."], "training": "Carpentry is a learn-while-you-earn trade. Most people start through a registered apprenticeship and log on-the-job hours under experienced carpenters, a path that typically runs three to four years, pays you from day one, and finishes with a recognized credential, no four-year degree and no student debt. Georgia offers several ways in, including registered apprenticeships and carpentry programs through the Technical College System of Georgia (TCSG).", "wages": {"gaEntryAnnual": "$34,890", "gaTopAnnual": "$63,470", "note": "Georgia range, from entry-level (roughly the lowest 10%) to top earners (the top 10%). Pay climbs with experience, certifications, and overtime, and many move into lead, foreman, or contractor roles.", "source": "BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (2025), via O*NET / My Next Move"}, "providers": [{"name": "The United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America - GA Campuses", "url": "https://www.carpenters.org/training_centers/ga/"}, {"name": "Southern States Millwright Local 1263", "url": "https://www.sectt.net/Module/Training/TrainingKennesaw"}]},
