    detail: {"dayInLife": ["Heavy equipment operators run the machines that move earth and build infrastructure, from excavators and dozers to cranes and graders. A day means operating with precision on site prep, roadwork, utilities, or vertical construction.", "It takes coordination, focus, and a feel for the machine, and skilled operators, especially crane operators, are highly valued and well paid on big projects."], "training": "Heavy equipment operators train through registered apprenticeships and specialized equipment and crane schools, typically one to four years depending on the path. Programs combine seat time on real machines with safety and certification, and crane and specialty certifications raise pay significantly. Georgia offers several ways in, including apprenticeships and heavy-equipment programs through the Technical College System of Georgia (TCSG).", "wages": {"gaEntryAnnual": "$37,810", "gaTopAnnual": "$62,840", "note": "Georgia range, from entry-level (roughly the lowest 10%) to top earners (the top 10%). Pay climbs with experience, certifications, and overtime, and many move into lead, foreman, or contractor roles.", "source": "BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (2025), via O*NET / My Next Move"}, "providers": [{"name": "Operating Engineers Local 926", "url": "https://iuoe926.org/?zone=/unionactive/view_page.cfm&page=Apprenticeship"}, {"name": "Heavy Equipment Colleges of America - GA Campus", "url": "https://heavyequipmentcollege.edu/campuses/georgia-campus/"}]},
