    detail: {"dayInLife": ["Safety professionals and inspectors keep people and buildings safe: reviewing plans, inspecting work against code, running site safety programs, and making sure every phase is done right. A day mixes field inspections with documentation and coordination.", "It suits detail-oriented people who know construction and want to protect the crews doing the work. Time on the tools is a real advantage here."], "training": "Many inspectors and safety professionals come up through a trade first, then add certifications (ICC code certifications, OSHA credentials, and state licensing for building inspectors). Others enter through technical or degree programs in construction or safety. It is a common, well-paid next step for experienced tradespeople.", "wages": {"gaEntryAnnual": "$46,070", "gaTopAnnual": "$100,450", "note": "Georgia range, from entry-level (roughly the lowest 10%) to top earners (the top 10%). Pay climbs with experience, certifications, and overtime, and many move into lead, foreman, or contractor roles.", "source": "BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (2025), via O*NET / My Next Move"}, "providers": [{"name": "Georgia Tech OSHA Training Institute", "url": "https://info.pe.gatech.edu/osha/", "note": "OSHA 30 / 500 / 510"}, {"name": "Board of Certified Safety Professionals (BCSP)", "url": "https://www.bcsp.org/", "note": "CSP / CHST"}], "providersNote": "Safety runs on certifications and experience. Most construction safety roles require OSHA credentials — typically OSHA 30, then OSHA 500 and OSHA 510 — paired with a few years of construction experience, or a bachelor's degree in occupational health and safety or a related field. Professional certification through BCSP opens the door to senior roles."},
